save(i,dict(title='Sum-of-squares automatability on the Boolean cube',formal='Historical question, resolved negatively: does adding all Boolean equalities x_i²=1 guarantee that a degree-d sum-of-squares certificate can be found in time polynomial in the size N of its explicit semidefinite program, whenever such a certificate exists, even if an additive error tending to zero is allowed? More precisely, is there a uniform algorithm A, a polynomial P and a rational error schedule η(N)≥0 with η(N)→0, such that on every promised input with a degree-d certificate of p(x)≥θ, A outputs within P(N) steps an explicit degree-d certificate of p(x)≥θ−η(N)? There is no such universal algorithm: the required output can have superpolynomial bit length, already when d=2.',definitions='''An input consists of n real indeterminates, rational polynomials p,q₁,…,q_m, a rational threshold θ and an integer degree bound d≥2. The intended feasible set satisfies q_j(x)≥0 and x_i²=1 for all i. Coefficients and exponents are explicitly written in binary. The source already asks the question for quadratic input polynomials, m=O(n), and integer coefficients of polynomial magnitude; the negative example fits these bounded-input conventions after a constant affine change of Boolean variables and clearing constant denominators. The intended feasible set is therefore a subset of {−1,1}ⁿ, possibly with additional constraints.

A degree-d certificate for p≥t is a polynomial identity p−t=σ₀+∑_j σ_j q_j+∑_i λ_i(x)(x_i²−1), where each σ_j is a sum of squares of real polynomials, each λ_i is an arbitrary real polynomial, and every summand on the right has degree at most d. Additional input equalities r=0 can be encoded as r≥0 and −r≥0; allowing arbitrary polynomial multipliers for those equalities is an at-least-as-strong certificate convention, and the published lower bound holds even with that convention. For degree two and quadratic equalities the multipliers are constants, so the conventions coincide.

The explicit semidefinite formulation uses coefficient matching in the monomial basis and positive semidefinite Gram matrices: σ=zᵀGz for a vector z of monomials and a symmetric matrix G with vᵀGv≥0 for every real vector v. N is the binary length of this explicitly constructed rational SDP, including the objective threshold and all coefficient-matching constraints. With the stated small input polynomials it has size n^{O(d)}. Running time is ordinary bit complexity, not a real-arithmetic operation count. The output must explicitly list finite binary rational coefficients of the Gram matrices and equality multipliers, satisfying the exact polynomial identity for the weakened threshold. The additive slack concerns the certified objective bound; arbitrary residual errors in the identity do not qualify. An arithmetic circuit or symbolic exponent tower standing for enormous coefficients is not the explicit certificate requested by the source.

The promise is existence of a degree-d certificate for the original threshold. The algorithm may behave arbitrarily on unpromised inputs. The error schedule is one fixed computable rational function of N, shared by all inputs, with limit zero as N→∞; the polynomial running-time bound is also universal. The negative conclusion is stronger than needed for this formulation: Section 5 supplies a family for which a fixed small positive additive slack still leaves coefficients requiring exponentially many bits. Thus no choice of vanishing error rate rescues the universal output algorithm.

The later counterexample uses Boolean variables w∈{0,1}. The substitution w=(x+1)/2 converts its constraints to the source’s {−1,1} convention, preserves degree, and changes bounded-degree coefficient bit lengths by polynomial overhead. With v variables, the family has degree-two certificates but requires doubly exponentially large coefficients throughout degrees up to a constant multiple of √v. Section 5.2 applies to a polynomial ε−∑_j w_{1j}+k for any fixed ε<1/2. Choosing, for example, an original ε=1/8 leaves the same obstruction after any eventual additive slack at most 1/8. This matches the source’s approximate-certification question as well as exact certification.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='The published negative answer supplies small-input Boolean systems admitting degree-two certificates whose explicit low-degree certificates need superpolynomially many bits, even after a fixed small weakening of the objective. This excludes any polynomial-time output procedure with a vanishing additive error schedule. It does not by itself settle the decision problem of whether such a certificate exists, or a model allowing succinct arithmetic-circuit encodings of coefficients.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Small semidefinite programs can require large answers',text='Counting the variables and constraints of a semidefinite formulation does not bound the binary length of a feasible certificate. The source isolates this obstacle for sum-of-squares optimization and asks whether Boolean equalities remove it.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Booleanity alone is insufficient',text='Raghavendra and Weitz answer the Boolean extension negatively in Theorem 2. Their example also resists attempts to remove the coefficient blowup by increasing the degree by any fixed factor, although the original certificate has degree two.',citation='solution'),dict(title='Positive criteria and decision questions remain distinct',text='The 2025 paper develops additional conditions under which low-degree certificates, including certain refutations, can be found efficiently. It recalls the general Boolean counterexample and separately asks about deciding fixed-degree refutation existence. Those remaining questions do not reopen the universal explicit-certificate claim.',citation='later')],why='Identifies a concrete limit on translating low algebraic proof degree into algorithmic efficiency. The resolution explains why coefficient encoding and output size must accompany degree bounds in claims about semidefinite optimization.',progress=[dict(date='2017',text='The ITCS paper raises the Boolean version of its approximate certificate-search question.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2017',text='Raghavendra and Weitz’s ICALP Theorem 2 gives a negative answer on Boolean systems, with a coefficient lower bound surviving constant degree increases and small objective slack.',citation='solution'),dict(date='2025',text='A polynomial-calculus criterion expands positive special cases, while the Boolean counterexample remains part of the stated background.',citation='later')],references=refs,status='resolved',status_note='Read the source’s exact output question and Boolean extension, the resolving Theorem 2 and Sections 5.1–5.2, and the 2025 follow-up’s distinction between certificate output and decision. Matched the Boolean encoding, degree, explicit bit representation and approximate objective. No complexity assumption is needed for this output-length obstruction; proofs were not independently audited.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Replaced an incomplete cross-reference with the original certificate-output task and its verified negative resolution. The vanishing slack is expressed by an error schedule; the stronger constant-slack lower bound makes the choice of rate immaterial to the negative answer. No unresolved SDP decision problem was substituted.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A fundamental limitation of degree-based claims about the computational power of sum-of-squares proofs, with direct relevance to bit complexity in optimization. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['The question asks whether Boolean constraints make low-degree sum-of-squares certificates efficiently recoverable.','The running time must include writing every coefficient in ordinary binary form.','Raghavendra and Weitz give Boolean examples with degree-two certificates but unavoidable enormous coefficients at low degree.','The obstruction survives a fixed small weakening of the objective, so allowing vanishing additive error does not help.','Later positive results concern additional structural conditions, while deciding certificate existence is a separate task.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','solution','later'])),notes=['Recovered the explicit certificate-output question, including approximate objective slack and degree.','Verified the Boolean counterexample and its constant-slack parameter, rather than inferring a decision-time lower bound from output length.','Explained the degree-preserving affine translation from 0/1 to ±1 variables and retained bit complexity.','Checked the 2025 follow-up and marked the historical question resolved.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs])
